[TEXT]
MID: 8679a0d58ec81d5931e8b5799de8941b
STATEMENT: >>>
The [LINK: DOC-ARCHITECTURE-BACKEND-READ-MODEL, backend read model] and [LINK: DOC-ARCHITECTURE-DECISIONS-OPTIMIZATION-BOUNDARIES, optimization decisions] define correctness constraints. This document owns how to measure improvements, not a second architecture or benchmark campaign status.

Use matched synthetic PostgreSQL workloads, disposable databases and exact baseline/candidate source and build identities. Measure current and pinned reads, cold construction and warm reuse, Knowledge/Evidence projection, tree/scene, search/statistics, history, mutations/receipts, and end-to-end navigation separately. Compare the same project hierarchy, body bytes, revision history, evidence density, concurrency, database settings, toolchain, instrumentation and cache conditions. Record counts of actual persisted records, not Git fixture file totals.

For backend work capture SQL query counts, rows and bytes, database and application wall time, transaction/lock duration, CPU, RSS, pool contention, projection bytes and growth. Capture cold and warm distributions with sample counts and raw measurements, and distinguish one-time seeding from operations. Include near-limit and rejected cases; timeouts and errors are not successful latency observations. Do not combine separate experiments into an aggregate speedup. Do not use real author data or change authentication, durability, scope or revision checks to improve a metric.

The [LINK: DOC-PERFORMANCE-PERFORMANCE-HARNESS, benchmark harness] documents runnable entrypoints and limitations. Its deterministic Git fixture is transport only; both Core and browser workloads run against the current PostgreSQL-backed product. The browser probe uses the authenticated Persistence API, current editor, IndexedDB recovery journal and PostgreSQL revision receipt. Historical Git-era measurements do not describe current PostgreSQL behavior. Actual browser/IME acceptance remains separate from performance probes and synthetic events.

Treat cold as first Core materialization in a fresh worker for a new project version. Database and host caches are not flushed. Compare exact source/build, fixture, database configuration, browser, sample counts and cold/warm conditions. Fixture setup, failed operations and timeouts are not successful workload latency. Store baseline manifests and summary results outside the repository checkout, or as explicitly retained CI/release artifacts. Do not commit raw measurement output. Keep large raw machine evidence in immutable release assets; do not turn a measurement baseline into a performance improvement claim.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
MID: 2dd7ece26461856173a8af7a28f826e2
UID: DOC-PERFORMANCE-PERFORMANCE-MEASUREMENT-retaining-new-measurement-evidence
TITLE: Retaining new measurement evidence

[TEXT]
MID: ebc703dddb6b3bebea7aa3eb51b6fb34
STATEMENT: >>>
Keep generated scene bodies, CPU profiles and bulky raw results out of Git commits. Retain the exact source/build identities, fixture plan and dataset manifest, per-file hashes, environment, summary and sample counts. The deterministic fixture generator at the recorded source must reproduce the recorded fixture identity and scene hashes; if it cannot, preserve the actual input bytes in the archive as well. Historical evidence is retained in immutable Git history and published release assets; do not rewrite historical revisions.

Use the existing archive packer with an explicit list of reviewed synthetic result files. Stage selected files under unique, flat basenames outside the checkout (prefix before/after filenames for matched runs), preserving the mapping to their original paths in the summary. Do not collect an entire workspace, Git directory, database dump, author data, credentials or model caches. Inspect and redact machine-local sensitive information before computing checksums; record redactions without altering measurements.

Example, with a new output directory and an immutable release asset URL:

```sh
node scripts/performance/artifacts.mjs inventory /tmp/evidence-recipe.json /tmp/reviewed-results \
  before-core.json after-core.json before-dataset.json after-dataset.json
node scripts/performance/artifacts.mjs pack /tmp/evidence-recipe.json /tmp/reviewed-results \
  /tmp/evidence-archive 'https://github.com/yohn-jp/codexia/releases/download/<evidence-tag>/evidence.tar'
node scripts/performance/artifacts.mjs verify /tmp/evidence-archive/INDEX.json /tmp/evidence-archive
```

Replace the example filenames and URL with the actual reviewed inputs and planned release tag (quote the URL in a shell). An authorized publisher uploads the archive without replacing an existing asset. Download it again and verify its archive and member hashes using the procedure below before publishing the final INDEX.json and summary in an immutable archive. A planned URL or locally generated manifest alone does not establish retention. If publication or verification fails, retain the local evidence and do not claim it is archived.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 92171abba016b3f35c51a41f28347447
UID: DOC-PERFORMANCE-PERFORMANCE-MEASUREMENT-immutable-historical-evidence
TITLE: Immutable historical evidence

[TEXT]
MID: 7eb6152135e6c8691667b77379b0e975
STATEMENT: >>>
Select the immutable archive manifest from the [archived wave0 evidence](https://github.com/yohn-jp/codexia/blob/91b6cb64a85da6c8f4b864a5843ac7fb776e08fa/evidence/performance/wave0/INDEX.json). Download the manifest before running the procedure below. Its storage record is authoritative for the release repository, tag, asset name, length and SHA-256; do not derive these from the current product name. Check the archive before extraction and validate each compressed and raw member using the existing artifact verifier. Run the following in Bash with GitHub CLI authentication for the repository named in the manifest:

```sh
set -e
index=/path/to/INDEX.json
evidence=$(mktemp -d)
asset=$(node --input-type=module -e '
  import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
  const { storage } = JSON.parse(readFileSync(process.argv[1], "utf8"));
  const url = new URL(storage.url);
  const match = /^\/([A-Za-z0-9._-]+\/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)\/releases\/download\/([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)\/([A-Za-z0-9._-]+\.tar)$/.exec(url.pathname);
  if (storage.type !== "github-release-asset" || url.origin !== "https://github.com" ||
      url.username || url.password || url.search || url.hash || !match || match[3] !== storage.file) {
    throw new Error("Invalid archive storage locator");
  }
  console.log(match.slice(1).join(" "));
' "$index")
read -r repo tag archive <<< "$asset"
gh release download "$tag" --repo "$repo" --pattern "$archive" --dir "$evidence"
node scripts/performance/artifacts.mjs verify-archive "$index" "$evidence"
tar -xf "$evidence/$archive" -C "$evidence"
node scripts/performance/artifacts.mjs verify "$index" "$evidence"
```

Retain immutable comparison inputs and raw evidence outside product documentation. A historical archive is not a current performance claim; specialized probes must bind their own runtime and artifact identities.
<<<

